If this directory is not yet a checkout of the worker's repo, make it one:

```
{{SETUP}}
```

Then check with `meow herd pair show {{PAIR}}`, and stop. A message will name each item to review.

{{WORKDIR}}

**Setting up a queue** ("set up a queue", or yes to your offer after exit 3). Ask at most three things, and only what you can't find out:

1. **Is the code repo public, or will it be?** For a GitHub `origin`, check first: `gh repo view <owner/repo> --json visibility -q .visibility`. Ask only if that can't answer.
2. **Where do work branches go?** Ask only when the repo is public (or will be), and only if you can't find out: once a work branch leaves this machine, does it go to the public repo, where anyone can see it, or to a private companion, or do work branches stay on this machine? Then offer the placement that fits:
   - a private repo (step 1 answers it; nothing to ask): the queue goes on its own branch on this repo's remote (`init remote <owner>`, which puts it on the `cat-herder-queue` branch; `--branch <name>` if the owner wants another name), so queue commits stay out of the code's history. Never put it on the branch the code is on: `init` refuses `--branch main`, even when the owner asks, since every claim and write would be a commit pushed there. If `meow` says a queue already lives on main, pass on its line about `meow queue move`, and move it only on the owner's word;
   - a public repo: the queue goes in a separate private repo, the companion, with the config kept out of the tree. Its URL comes from the owner; if they have nowhere, offer a bare repo on local disk (`git init --bare <path>`) as the simplest start. The answer picks one of three lines, each `init --local --companion dev=<url> <owner>` with a `--work` value:
     - they stay on this machine: nothing added (`--work local`, the default);
     - they go to the public repo: `--work origin`;
     - they go to the private companion: `--work companion`. `init` checks that the companion has the code's `main` and says what to run if it doesn't (`git push dev main`); never push the code yourself without the owner's word;
   - one machine only: `init --local ref <owner>`.

   If the owner wants cloud sessions (claude.ai/code) to work this queue, offer a placement they can reach, and say which it is: a private repo's own branch (the first) is reachable, once `queue.cloud.clone`, `queue.cloud.github` and `queue.cloud.remote` are set in `.git/config` (`init` does not set them); for a public repo, the companion has to be a private repo on the same git host, with the Claude GitHub App on it, not a path on local disk, and `--work` has to be `origin` or `companion`, since `local` starts no cloud session. The one-machine placement can't be reached from the cloud. For a private repo, also point them at "Cloud sessions: relay or direct" in the plugin's `../../docs/pairs.md` for the launcher's one setting; with a companion, the launcher's settings come from `--work`, so there is no setting to set. Before you push the branch a cloud session starts from to a public repo, run the plugin's `../../tools/dev-sidecar check <remote> <branch>`: if it exits 3, a rule refuses that push, so pass its offer of a private companion (`<repo>-dev`) to the owner, and run its `create <remote> <branch> --yes` only after they say yes.
3. **Who is the owner?** Default to their git user name as a slug; confirm it.

Then run that single `init`. It ends with `check:` lines (the queue it set up, working tree untouched, origin unchanged): show them. If this session was already working before the queue existed, offer a rescue: "Say 'rescue session' and I'll queue everything this session was tracking." (the `capture` skill's rescue session, also called Roundup, does it). When `init` ends with the voice offer (a new queue: its last line starts `meow: The herd speaks cat`), end your setup reply with that offer, word for word but without the `meow: ` prefix. The voice offer is made once, at setup, and never repeated; silence leaves it off. A yes runs the `herder` skill (or `meow admin voice on --force` on the owner's word); it is an offer, not a question, so the "unread, not a no" rule in this skill doesn't apply to it. If it refuses because the remote is a public GitHub repo, don't add `--public-ok` yourself; tell the owner and offer the private placement. If it wrote `.repoq`, remind the owner to commit it. If it found other Cat Herder repos near this one (`found N other Cat Herder repos near here`), show the owner those lines and offer, once, to peer with any they name; like the voice offer it is an offer, not a question, and silence peers nothing. Run the `meow peer find --add <names>` it printed only for the ones they name, since a peer is theirs to choose, and never run `meow admin upgrade` in another repo. After the voice offer, end the setup reply with one line, word for word: `run meow doctor once`. It reads the settings around Cat Herder, edits no file, and is offered at setup only. If the owner wants to type `meow` in their own terminal, give them the clone-based command under "Typing meow yourself" in the plugin's `../../docs/reference.md`, and run it for them if they say so.
